   115	## 2. Intrinsic geometry of the exact ring
   116	
   117	Put
   118	
   119	```text
   120	q=U^2-A-A^2Z.
   121	```
   122	
   123	The gradient is
   124	
   125	```text
   126	q_A=-1-2AZ,       q_U=2U,       q_Z=-A^2.
   127	```
   128	
   129	If `A!=0`, then `q_Z!=0`; if `A=0`, the equation gives `U=0` and
   130	`q_A=-1`.  Thus `Spec R` is smooth.
   131	
   132	There is one prime over `A=0`,
   133	
   134	```text
   135	P=(A,U).
   136	```
   137	
   138	At its generic point `1+AZ` is a unit and
   139	`U^2=A(1+AZ)`, so
   140	
   141	```text
   142	div(A)=2P.                                             (2.1)
   143	```
   144	
   145	After inverting `A`,
   146	
   147	```text
   148	R[A^(-1)]=C[A,A^(-1),U]
   149	```
   150	
   151	is factorial.  Nagata localization therefore says that `Cl(R)` is generated
   152	by `[P]`, and (2.1) says its order divides two.  The class is nonzero: if
   153	`P=div(h)`, then `h^2/A` has zero divisor and is a unit, so `A` would be a
   154	square up to a complex scalar in
   155	
   156	```text
   157	Frac(R)=C(A,U).
   158	```
   159	
   160	The ordinary `A`-adic valuation of this rational function field has value
   161	one on `A`, ruling that out.  Smoothness now gives
   162	
   163	```text
   164	Pic(R)=Cl(R)=Z/2<[P]>.                                 (2.2)
   165	```
   166	
   167	The same localization proves
   168	
   169	```text
   170	R^*=C*.                                                (2.3)
   171	```
   172	
   173	Indeed a unit becomes `c A^n` in `R[A^(-1)]`; regularity of it and its
   174	inverse along `P` forces `n=0`.
