   199	## 4. The automorphism character has no symplectic torus in its kernel
   200	
   235	## 5. Locally finite Hamiltonians are locally nilpotent
   236	
   237	Let `D` be a locally finite derivation of `R` satisfying `L_D omega=0`.
   238	Choose a finite-dimensional `D`-stable vector subspace `V` containing `1`
   239	and a finite set of algebra generators of `R`.  The analytic one-parameter
   240	subgroup
   241	
   242	```text
   243	exp(tD)|V
   244	```
   245	
   246	acts by algebra automorphisms of `R`.  Let `G` be its Zariski closure in
   247	`GL(V)`.  Multiplicativity and every defining relation are closed conditions,
   248	so `G` is a connected commutative linear algebraic group acting on `R`.
   249	Equation `L_D omega=0` gives `exp(tD)^*omega=omega`; this is also closed,
   250	so all of `G` preserves `omega`.
   251	
   252	If the torus part of `G` were nontrivial, it would contradict (4.3).
   253	Therefore `G` is unipotent.  Its Lie algebra acts nilpotently on `V`, so
   254	`D|V` is nilpotent.  Since `V` generates `R`, every element of `R` is killed
   255	by some power of `D`.  Thus
   256	
   257	```text
   258	D locally finite and volume preserving  =>  D locally nilpotent. (5.1)
   259	```
   260	
   261	Every Hamiltonian derivation is volume preserving by (3.2), proving the
   262	first implication in (0.2).
